<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu'izz al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">915–967</span> · Also known as Ahmad ibn Buya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu'izz al-Dawla was the youngest of the three Buyid brothers who built a Daylamite empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Born Ahmad ibn Buya, he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 945 and received his title from the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, whom he and his successors kept as a figurehead. He ruled Iraq as emir until his death in 967 and introduced public Shia commemorations such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Mu'izz al-Dawla" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27izz_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
